 ## Overview
-Error-Driven Gaussian Density Control (EDC) is a training-phase strategy that dynamically allocates model capacity and removes redundant primitives by accumulating and leveraging per-Gaussian statistics corresponding to sparse pixel errors [S1, unofficial]. It differs from sibling methods like [Hard Gaussian Splatting](10590-hard-gaussian-splatting.md) and [Error-Guided Gaussian Reallocation](13012-error-guided-gaussian-reallocation.md) by relying on online sparse pixel statistics and percentile thresholds for adaptive density control, rather than post-hoc dense error maps or fixed reallocation budgets [S1, unofficial]. This approach reduces computational complexity compared to dense error map approaches while improving reconstruction fidelity in challenging regions [S1, unofficial].
